<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Judaea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Judaea was the region of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, the land of the Jewish people in antiquity. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> its Jewish communities initially kept their ancestral law. The attempted suppression of Jewish practice by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV</a> triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabean-revolt">Maccabean revolt</a> in Judaea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/judaea.jpg" alt="Judaea" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davidbena · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giv%27at_Seled,_near_Tzafririm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Judea is a mountainous region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, part of which is desert, traditionally dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. It rises to 1,020 metres at the Hebron Hills, 30 km southwest of Jerusalem, and descends to as much as 400 metres below sea level in the east. Rainfall falls from about 600 millimetres around western Jerusalem to about 100 millimetres in the eastern parts, which form the Judaean Desert. Geographers divide it into the Hebron hills, the Jerusalem saddle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a> hills and the desert east of Jerusalem. The main north-south route ran along the top of the central ridge.</p><p>The name is a Greek and Roman adaptation of the Hebrew Yehudah, the name of one of the Twelve Tribes of Israel and later of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. The earliest known extra-biblical record of the name Judah is Nimrud Tablet K.3751, dated about 733 BCE. Under the Hasmoneans, the Herodians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> the term covered a larger area than before. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> described Judea as lying between Anuath on the north and a village on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the south, extending from the Jordan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the middle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the biblical account, a united monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> followed the settlement of the Israelite tribes, and scholarship is divided on the historical extent of such a kingdom. The northern kingdom fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> in 720 BCE. Judah paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from 715, later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and, after 601 BCE, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> conquered it in 586 BCE, destroyed the temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and exiled many of the inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE. Judea stayed under Persian rule until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s conquest in 332 BCE and later fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, until the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-maccabeus">Judas Maccabeus</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a>, which ruled for over a century.</p><p>Under the Hasmoneans Jerusalem grew greatly, and the number of farming villages around it nearly doubled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> besieged Jerusalem in 63 BCE during a civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcanus-ii">Hyrcanus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-ii">Aristobulus II</a>, and political rule passed to the Herodian dynasty as client kings. Judea came under direct Roman rule in 6 CE. A revolt began in 66 CE, and in 70 CE Jerusalem was besieged and razed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> destroyed. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt of 132 to 136 CE most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in Judea were killed or displaced, and Hadrian renamed the province <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria-palaestina">Syria Palaestina</a>.</p><p>Judea's decline ended in the fifth century CE, when it became a monastic centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> a major Christian pilgrimage site. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> grouped it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> and other districts as Palaestina Prima. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> took Jerusalem from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in 1099.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Judea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…traditional laws and internal government: Babylonian temple communities continued to operate under cuneiform legal traditions, the Jews of <b>Judaea</b> retained their ancestral law (until Antiochus IV's intervention), and Greek poleis kept their civic institutions, while a layer of royal authority above these arrangements extracted revenue and military service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Roman domination the city kept minting its own silver coins; Herod, king of <b>Judaea</b>, built it a theatre, and in the reign of Elagabalus it became a Roman colony, renamed Colonia Aurelia Pia Sidon in honour of its imperial sponsor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Roger Beck lists the places of the earliest phase (c. 80–120 CE): Rome, Nida and Mogontiacum in Germania, Pons Aeni in Noricum, Carnuntum in Pannonia, Novae and Oescus in Moesia, and Caesarea Maritima in <b>Judaea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>During and after the Babylonian exile, Aramaic, the common language of the Persian empire, became the everyday language of many Jews, but Hebrew continued to be spoken in <b>Judaea</b> for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Judea</b> was devastated, renamed Syria Palaestina, and Jews were barred from Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/state/bar-kokhba-state">Bar Kokhba state</a></li><li><blockquote>…Great's will named Archelaus as heir to the kingship, but Augustus in 4 BCE gave him only the title ethnarch and the heartland of the kingdom: <b>Judea</b> with Jerusalem, Samaria and Idumaea, about half of his father's territory, with the promise of a crown if he governed well.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-ethnarchy-of-judea">Herodian Ethnarchy of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
